			"id":"0df96daae9d701d9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Quantum Optics\n\n- [Quantum Optics](../2.%20Notes/Quantum%20Optics.md)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-720,
			"y":950,
			"width":380,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"3dc6f5e96077455f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Electromagnetic Optics\n\n- [Total Internal Reflection](../2.%20Notes/Total%20Internal%20Reflection.md)\n- [Light Polarization](../2.%20Notes/Light%20Polarization.md)\n- [Fresnel Equations](../2.%20Notes/Fresnel%20Equations.md)\n- [Light in Inhomogeneous Media](../2.%20Notes/Light%20in%20Inhomogeneous%20Media.md)\n- [Light in Anisotropic Media](../2.%20Notes/Light%20in%20Anisotropic%20Media.md)\n- [Birefringence](../2.%20Notes/Birefringence.md)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":440,
			"y":900,
			"width":500,
			"height":280
		},
		{
			"id":"721ee40ade52a2f7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Scalar field approximation - valid when polarization effects are negligible",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":570,
			"y":480,
			"width":240,
			"height":140,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"5108ffa5503a61ca",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Wave Optics\n- [Maxwell Equations](../2.%20Notes/Maxwell%20Equations.md)\n- [Electromagnetic Waves](../2.%20Notes/Electromagnetic%20Waves.md)\n- [Phase & Group Velocity](../2.%20Notes/Phase%20&%20Group%20Velocity.md)\n- [Diffraction](../2.%20Notes/Diffraction.md)\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":530,
			"y":-100,
			"width":320,
			"height":320
		},
		{
			"id":"ca41960642bf5dce",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Ray Optics\n- [Eikonal Equation](../2.%20Notes/Eikonal%20Equation.md)\n- [Ray Equation](Ray%20Equation)\n- [Fermat's Principle](Fermat's%20Principle)\n- [Paraxial Approximation](../2.%20Notes/Paraxial%20Approximation.md)\n- [Optical Power](../2.%20Notes/Optical%20Power.md)\n- [Imaging](../2.%20Notes/Imaging.md)\n- [Ray Transfer Matrices](../2.%20Notes/Ray%20Transfer%20Matrices.md)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-690,
			"y":-100,
			"width":320,
			"height":320
		},
		{
			"id":"24fbc109d51efde0",
			"type":"file",
			"file":"4 Misc/Attachments/ac8fa3ea8bd9b0c5e21c7e7f4d0f2a9ac37089e92dc54a0d83e097cee621b50b.png",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-294,
			"y":150,
			"width":734,
			"height":660
		},
		{
			"id":"19e564abe38df095",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$\\lambda\\to 0$ relative to system (obstacle) scale",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-57,
			"y":20,
			"width":260,
			"height":80,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"6bf64433c549bd64",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"When dealing with large number of photons (high intensity) and quantum fluctuations average such that the fields can be treated as classical rather than quantized.\n\nCan also be thought as $\\hbar\\to 0$ relative to the system's action (in analogy with the ray optic limit). ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-100,
			"y":860,
			"width":270,
			"height":360,
			"color":"5"
		}
	],
